; Deferred SHAPE checking: give every array DIMENSION its own named constant, then let the
; ASSEMBLER compare them. Demo for the "always define a constant" note in docs/ParkedFeatures.md.
;
; The parked multidim thread concluded there is no way to have both "write any calculation as a
; dimension" and "sound, complete shape equivalence" - because comparing unknown values forces
; SYNTACTIC comparison, which is the polynomial-identity problem. That is true at COMPILE time.
; At ASSEMBLY time the values are not unknown, so the comparison is a plain integer compare:
; sound, complete, and any expression allowed.
;
;   output/GAZLCmd docs/deferredShapeCheck.gazl main N 4 W 4   -> passes ([4][3] vs [N][W-1])
;   output/GAZLCmd docs/deferredShapeCheck.gazl main N 5 W 4   -> FAIL, names axis 0
;   output/GAZLCmd docs/deferredShapeCheck.gazl main N 4 W 9   -> FAIL, names axis 1
;
; Note the first case: neither Impala (it does not know N) nor today's signature-row validator
; (tools/gazl-validate.nuxjs.js `arraySignaturesCompatible`, a raw `a.size !== b.size` string
; compare) can accept [4][3] against [N][W-1]. The assembler can, because it has the numbers.
;
; Costs nothing at run time - every line here is a `!` directive. Code size is 2.

; Every DIMENSION gets its own named constant, so a shape is a tuple of symbols.
; N is host-supplied at assembly time - Impala never sees its value.

; --- array `a`, declared int a[4][3] ---
.d.a.0:			! DEFi #4
.d.a.1:			! DEFi #3

; --- array `b`, declared int b[N][W - 1] where N and W come from the host ---
.d.b.0:			! DEFi #N
			! SUBi <A> #W #1
.d.b.1:			! DEFi #<A>

; --- `f` takes an int[4][3]; the call passes `b`. Impala CANNOT decide this:
;     it does not know N, and `W - 1` vs `3` is a polynomial-identity question.
;     So it does not decide it - it emits the question for the assembler. ---
			! EQUi #.d.a.0 #.d.b.0 @.dim0ok
			! FAIL f(b): axis 0 is b[N] but f expects [4]
.dim0ok:		! EQUi #.d.a.1 #.d.b.1 @.dim1ok
			! FAIL f(b): axis 1 is b[W - 1] but f expects [3]
.dim1ok:		!

main:			FUNC
					PARA *1
				RETU
